WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.785
Welcome to the Concilium Security Risk Brief for Friday, October twenty third.

2
00:00:04.785 --> 00:00:13.959
Five minutes of security coverage from the last twenty four hours, to increase your situational awareness without decreasing your productivity.

3
00:00:13.959 --> 00:00:16.533
Here's what matters today.

4
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:18.488
We start with an update on Saudi Arabia.

5
00:00:18.488 --> 00:00:28.290
Reuters and AFP report, citing sources, that Saudi Aramco told its remaining staff to evacuate its headquarters in Dhahran, in the Eastern Province, amid continuing Houthi attacks.

6
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.451
There's no official confirmation yet.

7
00:00:30.451 --> 00:00:39.820
Canada now says to avoid all travel to King Fahd International Airport in Dammam because ongoing military activity is disrupting airport operations.

8
00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:53.290
And the TRAC terrorism account reports the Houthis are claiming missile and drone attacks on Abha International Airport, King Fahd airport in Dammam, the Aramco refinery in Yanbu, and the Saudi Ministry of Defense headquarters.

9
00:00:53.290 --> 00:01:04.600
In Sudan, the UN humanitarian office, OCHA, says air and drone strikes are killing civilians, including humanitarian responders, while funding shortages threaten the aid response.

10
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:12.857
The International Committee of the Red Cross reports a worrying increase in civilian harm from aerial attacks, with hundreds of people reportedly killed.

11
00:01:12.857 --> 00:01:26.739
And BBC Africa reports the UN human rights chief called a BBC investigation, which found evidence that the Rapid Support Forces systematically destroyed hospitals, deeply disturbing, saying the actions may amount to war crimes.

12
00:01:26.739 --> 00:01:32.775
In China, the US Geological Survey reported a magnitude seven point zero earthquake in Tibet.

13
00:01:32.775 --> 00:01:36.596
Reuters reports sixteen people killed and sixty five injured.

14
00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:43.865
In Kenya, TRAC reports al Shabaab militants carried out an armed assault on a Kenyan military base in Mandera County.

15
00:01:43.865 --> 00:01:49.651
TRAC's weekly watch also says al Shabaab has explicitly named Christians as targets in Kenya.

16
00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:53.119
This could have repercussions for ministries working in the area.

17
00:01:53.119 --> 00:02:00.509
In Europe, the State Department warns that violence and rioting tied to ongoing protests in France have occurred and may continue.

18
00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:15.207
The UK Foreign Office updated its advice for France, Reunion and Guadeloupe with information about demonstrations, and Canada updated its advice for Italy after demonstrations disrupted services and led to vandalism and clashes with police.

19
00:02:15.207 --> 00:02:17.568
A few updates on stories we've covered.

20
00:02:17.568 --> 00:02:27.096
Update on the Ebola outbreak: BBC Africa reports that both the Nigerian and Kenyan first cases passed through Uganda's Entebbe airport on October third.

21
00:02:27.096 --> 00:02:29.490
Africa CDC is investigating but says no link has been established.

22
00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:36.722
Reuters reports the Swiss government says the Nigeria patient did not travel to Congo.

23
00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:42.503
Update on Typhoon Champi: Reuters reports Taiwan shut down with hundreds of flights cancelled, and the storm is now approaching Japan.

24
00:02:42.503 --> 00:02:53.595
Update on Yemen: the Institute for the Study of War says the Houthis almost certainly captured a major road segment linking their front lines outside Taiz City to central Taiz Governorate.

25
00:02:53.595 --> 00:03:00.204
Update on Tigray, Ethiopia: ACLED says control continues to shift, with forces known as the TPF regaining the town of Abi Adi.

26
00:03:00.204 --> 00:03:04.089
Now a few quick notes by region.

27
00:03:04.089 --> 00:03:13.150
In the Middle East and North Africa, TRAC reports a security director in Hadhramaut, Yemen, survived a roadside bomb, and an appeals judge was assassinated.

28
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:20.980
The State Department spokesperson says the US is disturbed by developments at Libya's Central Bank, warning of risks to the country's financial and economic stability.

29
00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:37.621
Reuters reports Israel released almost all suspects in two settler attacks on Palestinians to house arrest, and an Israeli rights group has documented almost one hundred seventy incidents against the olive harvest in recent weeks.

30
00:03:37.621 --> 00:03:42.940
In Africa, TRAC reports an al Shabaab bomb at a militia checkpoint in Mogadishu, and a JNIM takeover of a militia post in southwestern Burkina Faso.

31
00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:51.287
BBC Africa reports police in Durban, South Africa, say a foreign national was beaten to death by a crowd of about fifty people.

32
00:03:51.287 --> 00:04:01.174
In South and Central Asia, TRAC reports an Afghanistan Freedom Front bomb attack on a Taliban intelligence unit in Takhar province.

33
00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:08.950
In Latin America, the State Department imposed visa restrictions on forty people in Bolivia and twenty one in Colombia over criminal networks.

34
00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:16.860
InSight Crime reports Bolivia sent two hundred thirty police officers to Santa Cruz in response to a wave of criminal violence.

35
00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:25.196
In Europe, Reuters reports Russia fired twenty one ballistic missiles at Kyiv in three waves, killing at least two people.

36
00:04:25.196 --> 00:04:32.988
And the World Health Organization says France has reported the first documented human case of Shamonda virus, which mainly affects livestock.

37
00:04:32.988 --> 00:04:44.409
On cyber, The Record reports Interpol arrested more than fourteen hundred suspected cybercriminals across Africa this summer, in a crackdown on online fraud, ransomware and business email compromise.

38
00:04:44.409 --> 00:04:47.085
That's today's brief.

39
00:04:47.085 --> 00:04:56.760
These security developments come from government travel advisories and trusted analysts, curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

40
00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:05.056
The brief summarizes public reporting and isn't a substitute for your own risk assessment, so check the original source before making travel decisions.

41
00:05:05.056 --> 00:05:07.199
This brief is digitally narrated.

42
00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:08.147
Questions?

43
00:05:08.147 --> 00:05:11.617
Reach Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

44
00:05:11.617 --> 00:05:13.576
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
